Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Blown Quartz Chokes the Shaft
- Story & Context: Deep inside Borehole 14, where the sulfurous damp pooled thick enough to rot leather, foreman Caleb Sterling noticed the air-flues rattling with an off-time, malevolent counter-rhythm that made the miners drop their shovels in terror. Suspecting a cave-in, Sterling crawled through a disused ventilation run only to discover three drillers sitting cross-legged around an antique glassmaker's carillon, pounding the sheet-metal intake with cold chisels while toasting into a copper horn. The resulting pressure wave blew out every safety lamp on the gallery and left the crew laughing maniacally in the pitch dark as the bass shook loose twenty tons of shale, immortalized by Vance's defiant opening cry: "Bite di pipe, mek di lung-box ring, quartz inna di throat fi di graveyard king!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro cinematic shot of a jagged shard of blown antique quartz glass embedded in a rusted iron ventilation duct, illuminated by a sliver of violent red subterranean flare light. Fine coal dust and moisture hover weightlessly around the crack, with the etched words "LUNG-BOX RING" ghosted onto the frosted glass surface against a backdrop of pitch-black dripping rock.

Track 2: "Who Cracked the Lamp-Prism?"
- Story & Context: In the auxiliary pumping vault beneath St. Just, technician Sister Morwenna confessed to her crew that she had deliberately smashed the optical beacon lens of the main pressure gauge just to hear the crystalline tinkling clatter bounce against the pneumatic ductwork. For forty-eight hours straight, as water crept up to the knees of the night shift, she cranked a broken music box playing a warped lullaby through an overdriven spring reverb while Vance hammered out an aggressive, relentless 1970s dancehall bogle rhythm on the steel bulkhead. Her confession became the track's spine, recorded directly through a mine telephone before the generator flooded: "Me cracked di prism fi hear di iron weep, so wine your waist down two miles deep!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Low-angle 1970s cinematic still of a broken beveled glass lantern prism lying shattered across iron floor grates in a flooded industrial chamber. The refracted light from a single dying tungsten filament casts stained-glass diamond patterns of sickly yellow and emerald green across water ripples and rusted pipe fittings.

Track 3: Krik-Krak Duppy-Wine
- Story & Context: When the Wheal Maria shaft was finally condemned and sealed with sixty tons of wet concrete in the autumn of 1979, rescue divers surveying the waterlogged air vents reported hearing a bizarre, syncopated metallic clanking still vibrating up through the pipe columns. Local lore claimed that the spirits of the drowned blasting crew had formed an undead sound system in the pocket of trapped air beneath the sump, perpetually locked into an unholy soundclash against the crushing hydrostatic weight. To this day, night-shift watchmen near the abandoned pithead swear they hear the detuned tines of a music box floating up through the drainage pipes alongside an eerie subterranean echo: "Water rise to di chin and di timber moan, but duppy a wine pon di foundation stone!"
